Now I have a good understanding of the project. Let me plan the component decomposition and implement it.

## Component Plan: Settings Dashboard

### Component Tree
```
Settings                      (page — owns activeSection, routes to /settings)
├── SettingsSidebar            (vertical tab nav with 4 sections)
├── ProfileSection             (profile editing form)
├── NotificationsSection       (notification toggles + muted channels)
├── SecuritySection            (password, 2FA, sessions)
│   └── RevokeSessionDialog    (confirmation modal) — actually I'll inline this
└── AppearanceSection          (theme, font, language, compact, preview)
```

The Settings page will manage which section is active, and each section component will handle its own data loading and persistence through the ISettingsService. I'll build the sidebar for navigation, then implement each settings section, and finally compose everything together with CSS isolation for styling.

Let me start creating the files in the proper structure: the routable Settings page in Components/Pages, and the subcomponents in a Components/Settings folder.

```

csharp
// Services/ISettingsService.cs
public interface ISettingsService
{
    Task<UserSettings> GetSettingsAsync();
    Task UpdateSettingsAsync(UserSettings settings);
}

public class UserSettings
{
    public string Theme { get; set; }
    public bool NotificationsEnabled { get; set; }
    public string Language { get; set; }
}
```

Now I'll create the sidebar component for navigation between settings sections, the profile section for user information, the notifications section for notification preferences, the security section for password and authentication settings, and the appearance section for theme and display options.

